### Check for existing issues Searched open issues for `x-litellm-response-cost`, pass-through spend and non-streaming cost tracking — no duplicate. ### What happened? A pass-through target that reports its own totals — `x-litellm-response-cost` and `x-litellm-total-tokens`, the contract in `upstream_usage_headers.py` for targets that *"invoke several models internally, so LiteLLM cannot price the request from the response body"* — has those values honoured on a **streaming** response and **discarded on a non-streaming one**. The spend row records a price LiteLLM derived from the response body instead of the one the upstream reported. Cause: a config pass-through whose path contains `/messages` is logged by `AnthropicPassthroughLoggingHandler` — `PassThroughEndpointLogging.is_anthropic_route` tests `"/messages" in url_route` and never looks at the host. On the non-streaming path, `_create_anthropic_response_logging_payload` recomputes and writes ```python kwargs["response_cost"] = response_cost logging_obj.model_call_details["response_cost"] = response_cost ``` with no `has_upstream_reported_usage()` check — unlike the `cost_per_request` path in `success_handler.py`, which does guard on exactly that. It is easy to miss, because the recompute sits inside one `try`: with a model name LiteLLM cannot price, `litellm.completion_cost()` raises, the whole block is skipped, and the reported values survive by accident. It only reproduces when the response body names a **priced** model. Streaming escapes for an unrelated reason: `endpoint_type` is derived from the target URL, which for a generic pass-through names no provider, so the Anthropic streaming handler never runs. ### User Flow **Before — a proxy admin routing to an upstream that prices its own requests sees wrong spend on half their traffic** 1. The admin configures a pass-through endpoint `/repro` pointing at that upstream, and restarts the proxy. 2. A developer sends `POST http://litellm-domain/repro/v1/messages` with `"stream": true`. The upstream answers and reports `x-litellm-response-cost: 0.777777`, `x-litellm-total-tokens: 4242`. 3. The admin opens `http://litellm-domain/ui/?page=logs` and sees that request at `$0.777777`, 4242 tokens. Correct. 4. The developer sends the same request **without** `"stream": true`. The upstream reports the same values. 5. The admin sees that request logged at `$0.000075` with 15 tokens — numbers the upstream never sent. Every non-streaming call is silently mispriced, and nothing in the logs says so. **After — both requests are recorded at what the upstream reported** 1. Same configuration. 2. Same streaming request → logged at `$0.777777`, 4242 tokens. 3. Same non-streaming request → logged at `$0.777777`, 4242 tokens. ### Proof the bug occurs Self-contained: a stdlib upstream that reports the two headers, plus LiteLLM and Postgres. No credentials, no real provider calls. <details> <summary><code>upstream.py</code></summary> ```python #!/usr/bin/env python3 """Minimal pass-through target that reports its own usage headers.""" import json import sys from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer REPORTED_COST = "0.777777" REPORTED_TOKENS = "4242" MODEL = "gpt-4o" # any model LiteLLM prices; use an unpriced name and the bug hides BODY = { "id": "msg_repro", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "model": MODEL, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "pong"}], "stop_reason": "end_turn", "usage": {"input_tokens": 10, "output_tokens": 5}, } SSE = "
".join( [ f'event: message_start
data: {json.dumps({"type": "message_start", "message": BODY})}
', 'event: content_block_start
data: {"type":"content_block_start","index":0,' '"content_block":{"type":"text","text":""}}
', 'event: content_block_delta
data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,' '"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"pong"}}
', 'event: content_block_stop
data: {"type":"content_block_stop","index":0}
', 'event: message_delta
data: {"type":"message_delta","delta":{"stop_reason":"end_turn"},' '"usage":{"output_tokens":5}}
', 'event: message_stop
data: {"type":"message_stop"}
', ] ) class Upstream(BaseHTTPRequestHandler): protocol_version = "HTTP/1.1" def log_message(self, *a): pass def do_POST(self): raw = self.rfile.read(int(self.headers.get("content-length") or 0)) try: streaming = json.loads(raw or b"{}").get("stream") is True except ValueError: streaming = False payload = SSE.encode() if streaming else json.dumps(BODY).encode() self.send_response(200) self.send_header("content-type", "text/event-stream" if streaming else "application/json") self.send_header("x-litellm-response-cost", REPORTED_COST) self.send_header("x-litellm-total-tokens", REPORTED_TOKENS) self.send_header("content-length", str(len(payload))) self.end_headers() self.wfile.write(payload) if __name__ == "__main__": port = int(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv) > 1 else 8099 ThreadingHTTPServer(("0.0.0.0", port), Upstream).serve_forever() ``` </details> <details> <summary><code>config.yaml</code></summary> ```yaml general_settings: pass_through_endpoints: - path: "/repro" target: "http://upstream:8099" include_subpath: true forward_headers: true ``` </details> <details> <summary><code>compose.yml</code></summary> ```yaml name: litellm-passthrough-repro services: upstream: image: python:3.13-alpine command: python /app/upstream.py 8099 volumes: - ./upstream.py:/app/upstream.py:ro db: image: postgres:17-alpine environment: POSTGRES_USER: litellm POSTGRES_PASSWORD: litellm POSTGRES_DB: litellm healthcheck: test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U litellm"] interval: 3s retries: 20 litellm: image: ghcr.io/berriai/litellm:main-latest command: ["--config", "/app/config.yaml", "--port", "4000"] environment: LITELLM_MASTER_KEY: sk-repro DATABASE_URL: postgresql://litellm:litellm@db:5432/litellm volumes: - ./config.yaml:/app/config.yaml:ro ports: - "127.0.0.1:4010:4000" depends_on: upstream: condition: service_started db: condition: service_healthy ``` </details> Run: ```bash docker compose up -d --wait # non-streaming curl -s -o /dev/null -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4010/repro/v1/messages \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-repro' -H 'content-type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"my-router-alias","max_tokens":16,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"ping"}]}' # streaming, same request curl -s -N -o /dev/null -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4010/repro/v1/messages \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-repro' -H 'content-type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"my-router-alias","max_tokens":16,"stream":true,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"ping"}]}' sleep 15 docker compose exec -T db psql -U litellm -d litellm \ -c 'select model, spend, total_tokens from "LiteLLM_SpendLogs" order by "startTime" desc limit 2;' ``` Observed: ``` model | spend | total_tokens -----------------+-----------------------+-------------- my-router-alias | 0.777777 | 4242 <- streaming: reported values kept gpt-4o | 7.500000000000001e-05 | 15 <- non-streaming: both discarded ``` Both responses carried `x-litellm-response-cost: 0.777777` and `x-litellm-total-tokens: 4242`. Set `MODEL` in `upstream.py` to a name LiteLLM does not price and the non-streaming row keeps `0.777777` — the recompute raises and is skipped. That is the `try` masking this. The proxy log for the discarding run also shows the recompute running: ``` anthropic_passthrough_logging_handler.py:297 - Error creating Anthropic response logging payload: ... ``` (only when the name is unpriced; with `gpt-4o` it succeeds silently and overwrites.) ### What part of LiteLLM is this about? Proxy — pass-through endpoints / spend tracking. ### What LiteLLM version are you on? `ghcr.io/berriai/litellm:main-latest`, image built 2026-08-12. The code path is unchanged on `main` as of `bc6e7df0` — `anthropic_passthrough_logging_handler.py` still has no `has_upstream_reported_usage` reference. ### Suggested fix Guard the recompute the way `success_handler.py` already guards `cost_per_request`: ```python if not has_upstream_reported_usage(logging_obj): kwargs["response_cost"] = response_cost logging_obj.model_call_details["response_cost"] = response_cost ``` …and the same for the token count, so a target that reports totals for the whole HTTP request owns both. Happy to open the PR with a test in `tests/test_litellm/proxy/pass_through_endpoints/test_upstream_usage_headers.py`.

## TLDR Problem this solves: - Bedrock managed batches could not be cancelled through the proxy - `POST /v1/batches/{id}/cancel` returned 400 without reaching AWS - The Bedrock job kept running and billing to completion How it solves it: - Batch cancel now calls Bedrock `StopModelInvocationJob` - Returns the refreshed job status after stopping - Maps `Stopping` to `cancelling` and `Stopped` to `cancelled` - Cancelling an already finished job returns its terminal status - Genuine stop failures still raise instead of being swallowed ## User Flow Before: the cancel call is rejected by the gateway, and the Bedrock job keeps running and billing to completion 1. An operator uploads a 100-record JSONL with POST https://litellm-domain/v1/files (multipart: `purpose=batch`, `target_model_names=bedrock-batch-haiku`, the file) and gets back a long scrambled managed file id 2. They start the batch with POST https://litellm-domain/v1/batches sending `{"input_file_id": "<that id>", "endpoint": "/v1/chat/completions", "completion_window": "24h"}` and get a batch id with `"status": "validating"` 3. They realize the batch was submitted by mistake and call POST https://litellm-domain/v1/batches/{batch_id}/cancel 4. The call fails with HTTP 400: `LiteLLM doesn't support bedrock for 'cancel_batch'. Only 'openai', 'azure', and 'vertex_ai' are supported.` 5. GET https://litellm-domain/v1/batches/{batch_id} keeps showing `"status": "in_progress"` until the job finishes, and every record is billed After: the same cancel call stops the job at AWS, and the batch reports cancelling, then cancelled 1. An operator uploads a 100-record JSONL with POST https://litellm-domain/v1/files (multipart: `purpose=batch`, `target_model_names=bedrock-batch-haiku`, the file) and gets back a long scrambled managed file id 2. They start the batch with POST https://litellm-domain/v1/batches sending `{"input_file_id": "<that id>", "endpoint": "/v1/chat/completions", "completion_window": "24h"}` and get a batch id with `"status": "validating"` 3. They realize the batch was submitted by mistake and call POST https://litellm-domain/v1/batches/{batch_id}/cancel 4. The call returns HTTP 200 with the batch showing `"status": "cancelling"` 5. GET https://litellm-domain/v1/batches/{batch_id} soon shows `"status": "cancelled"`, and the job has stopped at AWS ## Relevant issues Fixes #33986 ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-4854 ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [x] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Live proxy plus real AWS Bedrock model invocation jobs (us-west-2, `bedrock/us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0`, managed file with `target_model_names`), no mocks. Each leg uploads a 100-record JSONL, creates a batch, then cancels it through the proxy ### Before, at base `91aee78e78`: cancel is rejected and the AWS job keeps running ``` $ curl -sS http://localhost:41837/v1/files -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-qa-***" \ -F purpose=batch -F target_model_names=bedrock-batch-haiku -F file=@batch_input.jsonl {"id":"bGl0ZWxsbV9wcm94eTphcHBsaWNhdGlvbi9vY3RldC1zdHJlYW07...NGIzNw","object":"file","purpose":"batch","status":"uploaded",...} $ curl -sS -X POST http://localhost:41837/v1/batches -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-qa-***" \ -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d '{"input_file_id": "<file_id>", "endpoint": "/v1/chat/completions", "completion_window": "24h"}' {"id":"<unified_batch_id>","object":"batch","status":"validating",...} $ curl -sS -X POST http://localhost:41837/v1/batches/<batch_id>/cancel \ -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-qa-***" -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" {"error":{"message":"litellm.BadRequestError: LiteLLM doesn't support bedrock for 'cancel_batch'. Only 'openai', 'azure', and 'vertex_ai' are supported....","type":"internal_server_error","param":null,"code":"400"}} HTTP 400 $ curl -sS http://localhost:41837/v1/batches/<batch_id> -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-qa-***" {"id":"<unified_batch_id>","object":"batch","status":"validating","cancelling_at":null,"cancelled_at":null,...} $ aws bedrock list-model-invocation-jobs --region us-west-2 --max-results 5 --sort-by CreationTime --sort-order Descending \ --query 'invocationJobSummaries[0].[jobArn,status]' [ "arn:aws:bedrock:us-west-2:...:model-invocation-job/bbp12ydc1e3w", "Submitted" ] ``` The orphaned provider job had to be stopped out of band with `aws bedrock stop-model-invocation-job` ### After, at PR head `5764c0daed`: the same cancel stops the AWS job ``` $ curl -sS http://localhost:45374/v1/files -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-qa-***" \ -F purpose=batch -F target_model_names=bedrock-batch-haiku -F file=@batch_input.jsonl {"id":"bGl0ZWxsbV9wcm94eTphcHBsaWNhdGlvbi9qc29ubDt1...","object":"file","purpose":"batch","status":"uploaded",...} $ curl -sS -X POST http://localhost:45374/v1/batches -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-qa-***" \ -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d '{"input_file_id": "<file_id>", "endpoint": "/v1/chat/completions", "completion_window": "24h"}' {"id":"bGl0ZWxsbV9wcm94eTttb2RlbF9pZDoyZWU5...","object":"batch","status":"validating",...} $ curl -sS -X POST http://localhost:45374/v1/batches/<batch_id>/cancel \ -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-qa-***" -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" {"id":"<unified_batch_id>","object":"batch","status":"cancelling",...,"metadata":{...,"job_arn":"arn:aws:bedrock:us-west-2:...:model-invocation-job/h0p25rnpl6aw",...}} HTTP 200 $ curl -sS http://localhost:45374/v1/batches/<batch_id> -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-qa-***" {"id":"<unified_batch_id>","object":"batch","status":"cancelled",...} $ aws bedrock get-model-invocation-job --region us-west-2 \ --job-identifier arn:aws:bedrock:us-west-2:...:model-invocation-job/h0p25rnpl6aw --query '{status:status,message:message}' { "status": "Stopped", "message": null } ``` No out-of-band cleanup was needed: the proxy's cancel alone drove the Bedrock job to `Stopped` within seconds QA observations, not caused by this PR: - Cancelled batch retrieve leaves `cancelled_at` null; mapping predates PR - Batch retrieve shows `request_counts` total 0; predates PR - Before-leg 400 body says `internal_server_error`; path removed by PR ## Type 🐛 Bug Fix ## Caveats (if any) ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR

## TLDR Problem this solves: - include_subpath pass-through sub-paths reject virtual keys with a 401 - Only the exact path works, so budgeted keys cannot poll sub-path APIs - Forces the master key or leaving the proxy entirely How it solves it: - Treat sub-paths of an include_subpath endpoint like its exact path for auth - Auth-unset endpoints forward sub-paths for any valid virtual key - auth: true still enforces on both the exact path and sub-paths ## User Flow Before: a team proxies a provider whose resources live under a path, behind per-consumer budgeted keys, and every sub-path call is locked out 1. The proxy admin configures a pass-through endpoint at `/orx` with `include_subpath: true` and no `auth` flag, then restarts the proxy 2. A consumer sends POST https://litellm-domain/orx/rerank with their virtual key `sk-...` 3. The proxy replies `401 {"error":{"message":"Authentication Error, Only proxy admin can be used to generate, delete, update info for new keys/users/teams. Route=/orx/rerank. Your role=unknown ..."}}` 4. The same key on the exact path POST https://litellm-domain/orx is accepted, so only sub-paths are blocked 5. Another user could not reach any sub-path without being handed the master key After: the same virtual key is accepted on sub-paths, matching the exact path 1. The proxy admin keeps the same `/orx` `include_subpath: true` config and restarts the proxy 2. The consumer sends POST https://litellm-domain/orx/rerank with their virtual key `sk-...` 3. The proxy accepts the key and forwards to the upstream provider, so the response is whatever the provider returns, not a 401 4. With `auth: true` on the endpoint, an unauthorized key is still rejected on both the exact path and sub-paths 5. Another user now reaches exactly the sub-paths their key is allowed, and never needs the master key ## Relevant issues Fixes #36280 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Real proxy, real Postgres, real virtual key from `/key/generate`. The upstream target is `https://httpbin.org` because the defect is at the proxy auth layer, before any provider call, so the 401-vs-forward outcome is provider independent. A forwarded request returns the upstream status (405/503 from httpbin), never the admin-only 401. config.yaml ```yaml general_settings: master_key: sk-runci-master pass_through_endpoints: - path: "/orx" target: "https://httpbin.org" include_subpath: true headers: content-type: application/json ``` BEFORE, on unmodified base commit f6b9518ddb (fix absent) ``` $ VK=$(curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4000/key/generate -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-runci-master" -d '{}' | jq -r .key) $ curl -s -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4000/orx/anything -H "Authorization: Bearer $VK" -d '{"hello":"world"}' {"error":{"message":"Authentication Error, Only proxy admin can be used to generate, delete, update info for new keys/users/teams. Route=/orx/anything. Your role=unknown. Your user_id=*******","type":"auth_error","param":"None","code":"401"}} HTTP 401 # exact path already works for the same key (recognized, forwarded, not a 401) $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4000/orx -H "Authorization: Bearer $VK" -d '{}' HTTP 405 ``` AFTER, on this branch commit 7499c621ff (fix present) ``` $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4000/orx/anything -H "Authorization: Bearer $VK" -d '{"hello":"world"}' HTTP 503 # forwarded to httpbin upstream, the admin-only 401 is gone $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4000/orx -H "Authorization: Bearer $VK" -d '{}' HTTP 503 # exact path unchanged ``` ## Type 🐛 Bug Fix ## Changes `_run_centralized_common_checks` skips the centralized auth gate for pass-through endpoints whose `auth` flag is not `true`, so the upstream forward runs for a valid key. That carve-out matched only the exact configured `path`, so `include_subpath` wildcards fell through to the management-route branch and 401'd. A new helper matches an endpoint's exact path and, when `include_subpath` is set, its sub-paths (`route == path` or `route.startswith(path + "/")`), reading `auth` with the same `str(auth).lower() == "true"` spelling the route registration uses so `auth: true` keeps enforcing on both. ## Test verification (RED -> GREEN) Added `test_centralized_common_checks_include_subpath_pass_through_authz` in `tests/test_litellm/proxy/auth/test_user_api_key_auth.py`, parametrized over the exact path, a sub-path, a sub-path with `include_subpath` unset, a prefix-only collision (`/orxfoo`), and both `auth` spellings on exact and sub-paths. RED, reverting only the production change: ``` $ pytest tests/test_litellm/proxy/auth/test_user_api_key_auth.py::test_centralized_common_checks_include_subpath_pass_through_authz FAILED ...[endpoint0-/orx/rerank-False] - AssertionError: assert True is False + where True = bool(1) # the sub-path ran management-route authz -> the 401 1 failed, 7 passed ``` GREEN, with the fix: ``` $ pytest tests/test_litellm/proxy/auth/test_user_api_key_auth.py::test_centralized_common_checks_include_subpath_pass_through_authz 8 passed ``` ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR

## TLDR Problem this solves: - Bedrock batch settings break chat on the same deployment - Provider rejects the request as an unexpected field - Batch could only run on a batch-only deployment How it solves it: - Register the five batch fields as litellm params - Same list the agentic-loop and callback fields use ## User Flow Before: a developer whose app calls a Bedrock model gets a provider error on every chat request, as soon as the proxy admin configures that model for batch 1. The proxy admin adds the batch settings (role ARN, bucket, region) to an existing Bedrock deployment so the same model can also run batch jobs 2. The developer sends POST https://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions with `{"model": "claude-haiku-4-5", "messages": [...]}` 3. The response is HTTP 400. On Anthropic models it reads `aws_batch_role_arn: Extra inputs are not permitted`; on Nova, Llama and Titan it reads `extraneous key [aws_batch_role_arn] is not permitted` 4. Every chat and embedding request to that model fails the same way, so the model is usable for batch only and the admin has to stand up a second, batch-only deployment of the same model After: the same request succeeds, so one deployment serves both chat and batch 1. The proxy admin adds the same batch settings to the same Bedrock deployment 2. The developer sends the same POST https://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions 3. The response is HTTP 200 with the assistant's reply and real token usage 4. Batch jobs against that model keep working, so no duplicate deployment is needed ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-5598 ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [x] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Live local proxy, real Bedrock calls in us-east-1, real spend, no mocks. The `claude-haiku-4-5` deployment under test carries all five managed-batch fields (`aws_batch_role_arn`, `s3_bucket_name`, `s3_region_name`, `s3_output_bucket_name`, `bedrock_tags`); a second `amazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0` deployment carries the original three. Both legs boot the same config and differ only in the commit the proxy runs from Before, at the merge base 02b0ee7608: every non-batch request to the deployment fails ``` $ curl -sS -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" http://localhost:29764/v1/chat/completions \ -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: chat-ok"}],"max_tokens":16}' HTTP 400 {"error":{"message":"litellm.BadRequestError: BedrockException - {"message":"The model returned the following errors: aws_batch_role_arn: Extra inputs are not permitted"}. Received Model Group=claude-haiku-4-5 ..."}} /v1/responses -> HTTP 400 same "aws_batch_role_arn: Extra inputs are not permitted" error /v1/embeddings -> HTTP 400 "Malformed input request: 3 schema violations found" (titan flavor, one per leaked field) /v1/messages -> HTTP 200 (this route never leaked the fields) ``` After, at 3312f0beea: the same requests succeed on the same deployment ``` $ curl -sS -w "
